//! The reiny SDK.
//!
//! A launch publishes and subscribes by **naming a type and nothing else**. The type → topic
//! mapping is generated from `Reiny.toml` by `reiny-build` (each launch's `build.rs`) and embedded by
//! implementing [`Topic`] for every type. User code never touches a topic name (a string).
//!
//! ```ignore
//! use reiny::prelude::*;
//! use crate::publications::Ping;        // generated by reiny-build
//! use crate::dependencies::pong::Pong;  // likewise
//!
//! #[reiny::main]
//! async fn main(cloudy: Cloudy) -> reiny::Result<()> {
//!     let pings = cloudy.publish::<Ping>()?;
//!     let mut pongs = cloudy.subscribe::<Pong>()?;
//!     // ...
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! # Engines
//!
//! [`Cloudy`] reaches the bus through an [`engine::Engine`]. The default is zenoh
//! ([`engine::Zenoh`], feature `zenoh`). [`engine::Local`] is an in-process bus, so a launch can be
//! tested with no network at all — hand it to [`Cloudy::open`] through `RuntimeOptions::engine`.
//!
//! # The escape hatch
//!
//! zenoh features reiny does not wrap are reachable directly through [`Cloudy::session`]. The price
//! is that **zenoh is a public dependency of reiny** (`pub use zenoh`), so a zenoh major release is a
//! breaking change for reiny — past this point, zenoh's semver becomes yours.

